      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Solve the equation $3{y}^{2}-3y=2{y}^{2}+y.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-3y & =2{y}^{2}+y \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3y-(2{y}^{2}+y) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3y-2{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ (3{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2})+(-3y-y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\ny=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\ny=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=0,4.$",
